Q: WordPress REST API custom endpoint returns 401 Unauthorized even with logged-in user A: <pre><code>wp_localize_script('my-script', 'wpApiSettings', [
    'nonce' =&gt; wp_create_nonce('wp_rest'),
    'root'  =&gt; esc_url_raw(rest_url())
]);
</code></pre>
<pre><code>fetch(wpApiSettings.root + 'custom/v1/test', {
    method: 'GET',
    credentials: 'same-origin',   // or 'include'
    headers: {
        'X-WP-Nonce': wpApiSettings.nonce
    }
})
.then(res =&gt; res.json())
.then(data =&gt; console.log(data));
</code></pre>

Q: awk - &quot;transpose-like&quot; data transformation A: <p>As a starting point, here's a script that'll transpose your data and use <code>null</code> for any missing label, using any awk:</p>
<pre><code>awk '
    NR == 1 { next }
    !seen[$1]++ { dates[++numDates] = $1 }
    !seen[$2]++ { fqdns[++numFqdns] = $2 }
    { labels[$1, $2] = $3 }
    END {
        printf &quot;%s&quot;, &quot;DATE&quot;
        for (fqdnNr = 1; fqdnNr &lt;= numFqdns; fqdnNr++) {
            printf &quot;%s%s&quot;, OFS, fqdns[fqdnNr]
        }
        print &quot;&quot;
        for (dateNr = 1; dateNr &lt;= numDates; dateNr++) {
            date = dates[dateNr]
            printf &quot;%s&quot;, date
            for (fqdnNr = 1; fqdnNr &lt;= numFqdns; fqdnNr++) {
                fqdn = fqdns[fqdnNr]
                label = ( (date,fqdn) in labels ? labels[date,fqdn] : &quot;null&quot; )
                printf &quot;%s%s&quot;, OFS, label
            }
            print &quot;&quot;
        }
    }
' file
DATE www.site_1.tld www.site_2.tld www.site_3.tld
2025-12-01 high null null
2025-12-02 low null null
2025-12-03 mid low null
2025-12-04 high high init
</code></pre>
<p>Beyond that, you'll have to figure out how to implement your real requirements for when to print nil, null, etc. as it's not clear from the question what those requirements are and you didn't provide a <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/help/minimal-reproducible-example">minimal-reproducible-example</a> we could copy/paste to test with.</p>
<p>Pipe the output of the above to <code>column -t</code> to see it in a tabular format:</p>
<pre><code>DATE        www.site_1.tld  www.site_2.tld  www.site_3.tld
2025-12-01  high            null            null
2025-12-02  low             null            null
2025-12-03  mid             low             null
2025-12-04  high            high            init
</code></pre>

Q: Should I use std::rethrow_exception rather than directly rethrowing it? A: <p>I would assume that the IDE hint has been misinterpreted. I do not think that it wanted you to use a <code>std::rethrow_exception</code> construct, but to use a rethrow expression:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>try { do_stuff(); }
catch(my_exception_class&amp; e) {
    if (not can_ignore_exception(e)) {
        throw; // change made here
    }
    log_or_do_nothing(e);
}

</code></pre>
<p>And this form really has advantages, e.g. it maintains the original type of the exception.</p>

